@page "/trust"
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@using Portal.Channels
@inject Catalogue Catalogue
@inject IOptions<PortalOptions> Options
@inject Publishers Publishers
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Trust and signatures";
    // The project's key: the one that signed the first published version of Pkg.
    var pkgHome = Catalogue.Pinned().FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "pkg")?.Channel ?? "pkg";
    var pkg = Catalogue.Package(pkgHome, "pkg");
    // Pkg's key now: the maintainers' transfer if any, else the key of its first version.
    var firstKey = pkg?.Versions.OrderBy(v => v.Published).FirstOrDefault()?.Signer;
    var projectKey = Publishers.OwnerOf(pkgHome, "pkg", firstKey);
    var names = Publishers.Names();
    var earlier = pkg?.Versions.Select(v => v.Signer).OfType<string>().Distinct()
        .Where(k => !k.Equals(projectKey, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)).ToList() ?? [];
    var url = Site.ChannelUrl(Request, Options.Value, pkgHome);
    var site = Options.Value.PublicUrl.Length > 0 ? Options.Value.PublicUrl.TrimEnd('/') : $"{Request.Scheme}://{Request.Host}";
    var example = pkg?.Latest;
    var keyLine = projectKey ?? "";
}
<h1>Trust and signatures</h1>
<p class="lead">Every package here is signed by its publisher. Pkg checks the signature and every file on your machine, before it writes anything. This site holds no signing key: it cannot sign, change or replace a package, only refuse to publish one.</p>

@if (projectKey is not null)
{
    <h2>Pkg's key</h2>
    <p>Pkg itself is signed with this Ed25519 key@(names.GetValueOrDefault(projectKey) is { } owner ? $", the publisher {owner}" : ""). If it matters to you that the Pkg you install is the real one, compare it with the <em>Signer</em> that Pkg shows:</p>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@keyLine</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@keyLine">Copy</button></div>
    @foreach (var old in earlier)
    {
        <p class="note">Earlier versions of Pkg were signed by <a href="/publishers/@old">@(names.GetValueOrDefault(old) ?? Site.Short(old))</a> (<code>@Site.Short(old)</code>). A machine that installed one of them refuses the new key once, as Pkg should: accept it with <code>ACCEPTKEY @keyLine</code> after comparing it with the key above.</p>
    }
    <p class="muted small">Every key that signs packages here has its page: <a href="/publishers">Publishers</a>.</p>
}

<h2>What is signed</h2>
<p>A package version is described by its manifest: its name, version, CPU, dependencies, and every file with its size and SHA-256. The publisher signs the manifest. Since the manifest lists every file's SHA-256, one valid signature vouches for every byte of the package.</p>

<h2>What this site checks before it publishes</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Pkg's own checks.</strong> Every upload is checked by Pkg itself: each signature, each digest, each withdrawal.</li>
    <li><strong>A package keeps the key of its first version.</strong> A new version signed by another key is refused.</li>
    <li><strong>A published file never changes.</strong> An upload that would replace one is refused; a version can only be withdrawn by its publisher.</li>
</ol>
<p>What the site cannot tell you is who a publisher <em>is</em>: the key is the identity. The name shown beside it is a label the maintainers attached when they gave that publisher an upload key.</p>

@{
    var pol = Options.Value.Policy;
}
<h2>What this portal accepts</h2>
<ul>
    <li>@(pol.Push ? "Uploads with a key from its maintainers, checked as above." : "No uploads: this portal serves its channels read-only.")</li>
    @if (pol.Push)
    {
        <li>@(pol.BinariesAllowed
            ? "Binaries only from publishers the maintainers allow to upload them; every other publisher publishes by link: the signed manifest names an archive on an https server, and Pkg downloads it from there."
            : "No binaries at all: every publisher publishes by link, the signed manifest naming an archive on an https server that Pkg downloads and checks.")</li>
        <li>Archives @(pol.Hosts.Count == 0 ? "on any https server" : $"on these hosts only: {string.Join(", ", pol.Hosts)}").</li>
        <li>@(pol.NewChannels ? "A publisher's first push may create its channel." : "Channels are created by the maintainers.")</li>
    }
    <li>@(pol.PlainHttp ? "Channels answer over https and plain http." : "Everything is served over https only.")</li>
</ul>
@if (pol.Note.Length > 0)
{
    <p class="note">@pol.Note</p>
}
@if (pol.Contact.Length > 0)
{
    <p class="muted small">About these rules: @pol.Contact</p>
}
<p class="muted small">The same rules as JSON: <a href="/api/policy">/api/policy</a>. A refusal they cause names the rule, its value and this note.</p>

<h2>What Pkg checks on your machine</h2>
<p>At every install, upgrade, verify and repair: the manifest's signature, every file against the manifest, and that the signer is the key remembered for that package on your machine. The first version you install pins its key. A later version signed by another key is refused, with both keys printed. It may be a publisher who changed keys, or someone replacing a program under you; only you can tell, by asking the publisher somewhere you trust. If the new key is theirs, <code>ACCEPTKEY</code> followed by the new key accepts it, once. Pkg never decides that for you.</p>
<p>The connection proves nothing and does not need to: a channel read over plain http, from a USB stick or a shared drawer is checked the same way.</p>

<h2>Check a package yourself, without Pkg</h2>
<p>Three downloads and a verifier of about sixty lines of plain Python 3, following RFC 8032, that you can read first:</p>
@if (example is not null)
{
    var cmds = $"B={url}\n" +
               "curl -fsSO $B/index\n" +
               $"d=$(awk '$1==\"{example.Name}\" && $2==\"{example.Version}\" && $3==\"{example.Arch}\" {{print $4}}' index)\n" +
               "curl -fsSO $B/objects/$d.manifest\n" +
               "curl -fsSO $B/objects/$d.sig\n" +
               $"curl -fsSO {site}/verify-manifest.py\n" +
               $"python3 verify-manifest.py $d.manifest $d.sig {example.Signer}";
    <pre><code>@cmds</code></pre>
    <p class="muted small">It says <code>the manifest is what its publisher signed</code>, or <code>do not trust this package</code> when a single byte differs. The manifest's <code>File:</code> lines then give each file's SHA-256 to compare with <code>shasum -a 256</code>.</p>
}

<h2>When a publisher loses a key</h2>
<p>There is no recovery, and this site cannot re-sign anything. The publisher makes a new key, says so where their users can see it, and signs the next version with it; the maintainers move that publisher's upload permission to the new key. Every machine that has the package refuses the new key once, and its owner accepts it with <code>ACCEPTKEY</code>. Nothing already installed is affected.</p>

<p>The full document, with the exact records and exit codes: <a href="/docs/signing">Signatures and trust</a>.</p>
